,"/blog/2019-02-12-anglesharp-v011-released"],"release":["/blog/2015-08-27-anglesharp-v09-released","/blog/2015-09-16-anglesharp-v091-released","/blog/2015-09-24-anglesharp-v092-released","/blog/2015-10-12-anglesharp-v093-released","/blog/2015-12-31-anglesharp-v094-released","/blog/2016-03-17-anglesharp-v95-released","/blog/2016-05-06-anglesharp-v96-released","/blog/2016-07-16-anglesharp-v97-released","/blog/2016-09-04-anglesharp-v098-released","/blog/2016-10-03-anglesharp-scripting-v050-released","/blog/2018-01-03-anglesharp-v099-released","/blog/2019-01-06-anglesharp-v010-released","/blog/2019-02-12-anglesharp-v011-released"],"released":["/blog/2015-08-27-anglesharp-v09-released","/blog/2015-09-16-anglesharp-v091-released","/blog/2015-09-24-anglesharp-v092-released","/blog/2015-10-12-anglesharp-v093-released","/blog/2015-12-31-anglesharp-v094-released","/blog/2016-03-17-anglesharp-v95-released","/blog/2016-05-06-anglesharp-v96-released","/blog/2016-07-16-anglesharp-v97-released","/blog/2016-09-04-anglesharp-v098-released","/blog/2016-10-03-anglesharp-scripting-v050-released","/blog/2018-01-03-anglesharp-v099-released","/blog/2019-01-06-anglesharp-v010-released","/blog/2019-02-12-anglesharp-v011-released"],"n":["/blog/2018-03-09-taking-anglesharp-to-the-next-level"],"ne":["/blog/2018-03-09-taking-anglesharp-to-the-next-level"],"nex":["/blog/2018-03-09-taking-anglesharp-to-the-next-level"],"next":["/blog/2018-03-09-taking-anglesharp-to-the-next-level"],"l":["/blog/2018-03-09-taking-anglesharp-to-the-next-level"],"le":["/blog/2018-03-09-taking-anglesharp-to-the-next-level"],"lev":["/blog/2018-03-09-taking-anglesharp-to-the-next-level"],"leve":["/blog/2018-03-09-taking-anglesharp-to-the-next-level"],"level":["/blog/2018-03-09-taking-anglesharp-to-the-next-level"],"f":["/blog/2019-05-02-dotnet-foundation","/blog/2019-05-06-anglesharp-v012-forward"],"fo":["/blog/2019-05-02-dotnet-foundation"],"fou":["/blog/2019-05-02-dotnet-foundation"],"foun":["/blog/2019-05-02-dotnet-foundation"],"found":["/blog/2019-05-02-dotnet-foundation"],"founda":["/blog/2019-05-02-dotnet-foundation"],"foundat":["/blog/2019-05-02-dotnet-foundation"],"foundati":["/blog/2019-05-02-dotnet-foundation"],"foundatio":["/blog/2019-05-02-dotnet-foundation"],"foundation":["/blog/2019-05-02-dotnet-foundation"],"t":["/blog/2019-05-06-anglesharp-v012-forward"],"th":["/blog/2019-05-06-anglesharp-v012-forward"],"the":["/blog/2019-05-06-anglesharp-v012-forward"],"fu":["/blog/2019-05-06-anglesharp-v012-forward"],"fut":["/blog/2019-05-06-anglesharp-v012-forward"],"futu":["/blog/2019-05-06-anglesharp-v012-forward"],"futur":["/blog/2019-05-06-anglesharp-v012-forward"],"future":["/blog/2019-05-06-anglesharp-v012-forward"]},{},{},{},{},{},{}],{},["@/blog/2015-08-27-anglesharp-v09-released","@/blog/2015-08-29-anglesharp-documentation","@/blog/2015-09-16-anglesharp-v091-released","@/blog/2015-09-24-anglesharp-v092-released","@/blog/2015-10-04-anglesharp-io","@/blog/2015-10-12-anglesharp-v093-released","@/blog/2015-12-31-anglesharp-v094-released","@/blog/2016-03-17-anglesharp-v95-released","@/blog/2016-05-06-anglesharp-v96-released","@/blog/2016-07-16-anglesharp-v97-released","@/blog/2016-09-04-anglesharp-v098-released","@/blog/2016-10-03-anglesharp-scripting-v050-released","@/blog/2018-01-03-anglesharp-v099-released","@/blog/2018-03-09-taking-anglesharp-to-the-next-level","@/blog/2019-01-06-anglesharp-v010-released","@/blog/2019-02-12-anglesharp-v011-released","@/blog/2019-05-02-dotnet-foundation","@/blog/2019-05-06-anglesharp-v012-forward","@/blog/2019-05-15-anglesharp-v0121","@/blog/2020-03-13-anglesharp-v0140","@/blog/2026-07-25-anglesharp-css-v1","@/blog/2026-07-27-anglesharp-v160","@/blog/2026-08-01-anglesharp-js-v100-release","@/blog/2026-11-06-anglesharp-v110-release","@/general/introduction","@/projects/Overview","@/docs/01-articles","@/docs/02-samples"]],{"/blog/2015-08-27-anglesharp-v09-released":{"title":"AngleSharp v0.9 released","date":"2015-08-27 14:05:00","categories":"news release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2015-08-27-anglesharp-v09-released","keywords":["title","date","categories","news","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl","section"],"link":"/blog/2015-08-27-anglesharp-v09-released","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp v0.9 released\"\ndate: 2015-08-27 14:05:00\ncategories: news release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nYesterday the latest version of Anglesharp has been released. This release marks the v0.9 milestone. Besides providing skeleton implementations for, e.g., the recent shadow DOM API draft, the `picture` element, etc., this version fixes some bugs that may appear in conjunction with using scripts.\n\nScripts are one of the success stories for AngleSharp. They make this library so useful. Therefore the AngleSharp.Scripting project is moving forward as well. The *AngleSharp.Scripting.JavaScript* library was yesterday released in with version 0.3. Here we will now try to align with the versioning of AngleSharp.Core.\n\nAngleSharp.Core will definitely be split up. The library is already too huge and contains too many features to be considered lightweight. Let's have a look at the SLOC (taken two months ago) of the DOM part alone:\n\n![AngleSharp Core DOM SLOC Distribution](../../images/dist_dom.png)\n\nFrom this picture alone we can already estimate that splitting the library could be benificial. We have roughly 1/3 SLOC spent on general, HTML and CSS functionality. The splitting could therefore result in three or four parts:\n\n* *AngleSharp.Core.Common*, containing the basic infrastructure and definitions [no dependency]\n* *AngleSharp.Core.Html*, containing the HTML parser and DOM implementation [depending on Common]\n* *AngleSharp.Core.Css*, containing the CSS parser and CSSOM implementation [depending on Common]\n* *AngleSharp.Core.Complete*, aggregating the Core and providing further helpers [depending on the former three]\n\nExperiments with a proper dissection will begin soon. Also the renderer part will then be discussed. Plans have already been made and it seems likely that a renderer will be published within this year (experimental stage). Here a new project, AngleSharp.Renderer will be opened. The renderer itself will contain many libraries, specifically to make the renderer common infrastructure a PCL again with specific platform libraries that contain the actual drawing code."},"/blog/2015-08-29-anglesharp-documentation":{"title":"AngleSharp Documentation","date":"2015-08-29 10:01:00","categories":"information documentation","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2015-08-29-anglesharp-documentation","keywords":["title","categories","information","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl","section","posts"],"link":"/blog/2015-08-29-anglesharp-documentation","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp Documentation\"\ndate: 2015-08-29 10:01:00\ncategories: information documentation\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nAn important aspect that is still missing (on the new homepage) is documentation. There should be two directly linked pages: \"Get Started\" and \"Documentation\". Right now the documentation can only be found in the Wiki, with a little code being displayed in the *AngleSharp/AngleSharp* repository. My plan is to keep both, but to update / sync them from a larger (more dedicated) source.\n\nI am actively working on a documentation system, which allows deployment in several formats. Here we have HTML, Markdown and LaTeX. However, it is not that the markup system converts contents into other markup systems, which are directly or indirectly bound to output devices, but rather that this system allows very structured and programmatic access. It has LaTeX like features (more lightweight of course) in a RST-like syntax. The syntax has not yet stabilized, which is why I am using XML (parsed with AngleSharp of course) for tests. Finally the idea is to have one `AngleSharp/Documentation` repository containing the documentation code, which is then produced and placed (probably automatically) in the Wiki and on the page. Also a PDF (kind of like a book) will be produced from the documentation.\n\nOne of the most important features of this documentation system will be the ability to embed code from external files. These files may be given by URLs. The idea is to place the snippets shown in the documentation in unit tests. The unit tests are run in their respective repository (tests of core functionality in `AngleSharp.Core`, tests of scripting functionality in `AngleSharp.Scripting`). That ensures that the code shown in the documentation is always up to date and compilable. Of course one can specify certain lines, ranges or delimiters to limit the code shown in the documentation.\n\nThe only remaining question is: When will this documentation system be available? Well, I can't give an exact date, but it has to be prior to v1.0. I hope to finish the system's software in the end of October and to finish the (full!) documentation itself in the end of November."},"/blog/2015-09-16-anglesharp-v091-released":{"title":"AngleSharp v0.9.1 released","date":"2015-09-16 11:08:00","categories":"news release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2015-09-16-anglesharp-v091-released","keywords":["title","date","categories","news","release","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl"],"link":"/blog/2015-09-16-anglesharp-v091-released","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp v0.9.1 released\"\ndate: 2015-09-16 11:08:00\ncategories: news release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nA week ago the the first patch for AngleSharp v0.9.1 has been released. Besides fixing some issues the event loop model has been reworked. This will not be the last update to this mechanism. The next update v0.9.2 will focus on closing some existing issues, such as the proposed `CssNode`.\n\nOne of the most important additions to AngleSharp v0.9.1 is the ability to filter (http, data, ...) requests. The standard requester service has been extended to provide the ability for this integration. It is therefore possible to stop unwanted requests directly without having to provide a custom `IRequester` implementation.\n\nFinally a remark for the upcoming releases: The service model will definitely change until v1. Right now there are too many interfaces and layers required to extend AngleSharp. Also the whole approach won't scale well once loosely coupled abilities, such as drawing or performance capturing, arrive. I believe that methods may be happier if they can just send a message, which may or may not find a listener. These messages will be very loosely coupled.\n\nAt the moment I don't know if such a potential change will also affect the event aggregator, but only using DOM events may actually be a good thing. In the end the `BrowsingContext` itself may end up as an `EventTarget`, which would bring consistency across the entire library."},"/blog/2015-09-24-anglesharp-v092-released":{"title":"AngleSharp v0.9.2 released","date":"2015-09-24 23:44:00","categories":"news release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2015-09-24-anglesharp-v092-released","keywords":["title","anglesharp","released","date","categories","news","release","author","florian","rappl"],"link":"/blog/2015-09-24-anglesharp-v092-released","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp v0.9.2 released\"\ndate: 2015-09-24 23:44:00\ncategories: news release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nThis week's minor update was only a small patch that fixed a bug in the tokenizer and improved the XML parser's performance. It also features the brandnew `application/json` encoding type for form submission. The form submission process internals have been redesigned to be much easier to extend and use. The `FormDataSet` and `FormDataSetEntry` classes are now public. This forms the basis for sending forms without requiring a webpage or valid `<form>` element at all.\n\nThe renewed CSSOM is not part of v0.9.2. Also the previously mentioned update to the service model and the event aggregator change could not make it. They will (at least partially, hopefully) find their way into v0.9.3 or some update afterwards.\n\nUntil this point more and more updates are being done via feature branches. From now on this will be the only model. Every new feature or update has to be merged to `devel` from a specific feature branch, which is associated with an issue. The idea is to make the development as transparent and open as possible. Also future contributions, discussions, and general user engagement should be boosted."},"/blog/2015-10-04-anglesharp-io":{"title":"AngleSharp IO","date":"2015-10-04 23:34:00","categories":"news project","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2015-10-04-anglesharp-io","keywords":["title","anglesharp","date","categories","news","project","author","florian","rappl","userid"],"link":"/blog/2015-10-04-anglesharp-io","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp IO\"\ndate: 2015-10-04 23:34:00\ncategories: news project\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nIn the last days one of the remaining projects was officially launched: `AngleSharp.Io`. This library will provide many essential IO classes, helper methods, and DOM interfaces. Most importantly it will bring new / improved requesters, such as a much better HTTP/HTTPS requester build on top of the `HttpClient` class. As a consequence this library will unfortunately not be released as a PCL. In the long run more requesters will be integrated.\n\n`AngleSharp.Io` is also the library that will finally offer a `WebSocket` implementation. Also the `Storage` interface will be made available, which can then be instantied as `localStorage` or `sessionStorage`. Under the hood the library should also be able to handle caching or resources.\n\nRight now the work has just begun. The first work items only focus on the requester side, with a few interesting items, e.g., `WebSocket`, being on the way. As with the (JavaScript) scripting library the exact roadmap is unclear at this point in time, however, we will try to come up with one in the next couple of months."},"/blog/2015-10-12-anglesharp-v093-released":{"title":"AngleSharp v0.9.3 released","date":"2015-10-12 11:44:00","categories":"news release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2015-10-12-anglesharp-v093-released","keywords":["title","released","date","categories","news","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl"],"link":"/blog/2015-10-12-anglesharp-v093-released","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp v0.9.3 released\"\ndate: 2015-10-12 11:44:00\ncategories: news release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nAngleSharp v0.9.3 is another round of minor updates. Besides a few bug fixes the `CompareDocumentPosition` method has been improved. It now passes all tests and works reliably.\n\nThe most interesting new feature is the ability to define custom handling of entities. This can be done via the `IEntityService`. The `GetSymbol` is usually called with an entity like `gt` for XML or `gt;` for HTML. The difference between XML and HTML lies in the way that HTML handles entity errors. HTML has the possibility to use non-semicolon-terminated entities.\n\nThe simplest way would be to use, e.g., `XmlEntityService.Resolver`, in a custom implementation. That way the common entities would be resolved by the already available service.\n\nThe next release will most probably be a feature release again. Hopefully features such as the CSSOM improvements, factory extensions, or a virtual file system, will be integrated.\n"},"/blog/2015-12-31-anglesharp-v094-released":{"title":"AngleSharp v0.9.4 released","date":"2015-12-31 08:36:00","categories":"news release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2015-12-31-anglesharp-v094-released","keywords":["title","released","date","categories","news","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl"],"link":"/blog/2015-12-31-anglesharp-v094-released","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp v0.9.4 released\"\ndate: 2015-12-31 08:36:00\ncategories: news release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nFinally, after weeks of delays and many discussions, AngleSharp v0.9.4 is available. There have been some bug fixes and improvements. Most importantly, these are encoding and insertion pointer fixes.\n\nHowever, AngleSharp v0.9.4 is in fact more than just a minor release. It could be considered a bridge release, as many internal things have been changed for the better. The upcoming version(s) will continue to walk the path enabled by v0.9.4.\n\nFeatures, such as the behavior of `OpenAsync` (delayed until embedded resources finished loading) or the virtual file system are already partially available. The CSSOM will also see more updates and will be enhanced with further helpers to modify the OM with objects instead of raw strings.\n\nOn the API side a lot new things will come up. Many internal concepts will be made public and the parsing can be expected to become even more flexible.\n\nLast but not least, the performance will be improved. While HTML is already quite decent (but could be improved in some scenarios), CSS has plenty of room for improvement.\n"},"/blog/2016-03-17-anglesharp-v95-released":{"title":"AngleSharp v0.9.5 released","date":"2016-03-17 14:44:00","categories":"news release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2016-03-17-anglesharp-v95-released","keywords":["title","date","categories","news","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl","section"],"link":"/blog/2016-03-17-anglesharp-v95-released","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp v0.9.5 released\"\ndate: 2016-03-17 14:44:00\ncategories: news release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nIt has been quite a while until the last release, but better late than never: AngleSharp v0.9.5 is now available. There have been some bug fixes and improvements. Most importantly (and thanks to [Jeremy Meng](https://github.com/jeremymeng) from Microsoft after discussions with the core Nancy developers) we now support the CoreCLR (*dotnet*) target via NuGet.\n\nThe focus of v0.9.5 was to fix critical bugs, improve parts of the API, and rewrite the internal CSSOM representation. The internal CSSOM changes are not complete yet, but sufficient for v0.9.5 to be released. The critical bugs involved mostly encoding related issues.\n\nPreviously, a virtual file system for AngleSharp has been announced. This feature was essentially abandoned as it would be more appropriate in AngleSharp.Io. AngleSharp provides everything to keep track of requests / responses. The internal system has been adjusted and rewritten to cover all cases.\n\nAngleSharp v0.9.5 comes with a set of new helpers. Some of these helpers are CSSOM related, others are there to distinguish between the different types of stylesheets. There are also helpers that are similar to special jQuery filters (some of which did not have any LINQ-counterpart already).\n\nThe development focus in the next couple of weeks will be of course on v0.9.6, however, the extension libraries will be adjusted to v0.9.5 first. Especially AngleSharp.Scripting.JavaScript did not get the (dev) attention it requires and deserves. This will be addressed first.\n\nThanks to everyone for their contribution!"},"/blog/2016-05-06-anglesharp-v96-released":{"title":"AngleSharp v0.9.6 released","date":"2016-05-06 13:27:00","categories":"news release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2016-05-06-anglesharp-v96-released","keywords":["title","released","date","categories","news","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl"],"link":"/blog/2016-05-06-anglesharp-v96-released","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp v0.9.6 released\"\ndate: 2016-05-06 13:27:00\ncategories: news release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nMore than two months after the release of AngleSharp v0.9.5 the new version v0.9.6 has been published. Besides some bug fixes the version focused on starting some changes regarding the final API touches and modifications.\n\nThe version may contain breaking changes to anyone using AngleSharp. Two of things that will potentially break builds are concerned with API renaming (e.g., `Submit` becomes `SubmitAsync`) and the removal of the `IEventAggregator`. While the former can be solved easily, the second one may actually require a little bit more time. Dropping the `IEventAggregator` was necessary to provide a uniform API that can also be exposed in JavaScript.\n\nAdditionally, the usage of `new Configuration()` is highly *discouraged*. If you create plain new configurations **no factories** will be available to parser, requesters, ... - a lot of components. This will result in ill-behavior. I advise everyone to use `Configuration.Default` as the basis for any configuration adjustments.\n\nFinally, the scripting library (`AngleSharp.Scripting.JavaScript`) has been updated - supporting the latest version of the core AngleSharp library and bringing some fixes and improvements. Together with some changes in the core library the experience should be much more complete right now, but there is still some way to go.\n\nThe samples, demo projects, and the `AngleSharp.Io` extension library have been updated accordingly. They all work seamlessly together and will be unified with the release of v1.0.0, planned later this year.\n\nThanks to everyone for their contribution!"},"/blog/2016-07-16-anglesharp-v97-released":{"title":"AngleSharp v0.9.7 released","date":"2016-07-16 22:20:00","categories":"news release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2016-07-16-anglesharp-v97-released","keywords":["title","released","date","categories","news","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl"],"link":"/blog/2016-07-16-anglesharp-v97-released","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp v0.9.7 released\"\ndate: 2016-07-16 22:20:00\ncategories: news release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nToo much time has past since the last update. The current release v0.9.7 marks the beginning of a dramatic change in the AngleSharp philosophy. For all related projects the target will change with the upcoming version. AngleSharp will be fully targeting .NET core. This change is an investment in the future of .NET core. Furthermore, it should simplify the development process in the long run - as the target platform is clearly defined.\n\nAnother upcoming change is related to the DOM interfacing. Right now AngleSharp tries to portray the IDL from the W3C in form of .NET interfaces. To make some things (e.g., different naming conventions) possible attributes have been inserted. However, the major problem with these interfaces is the coupling they are introducing. It's not the most plugin friendly mechanism, as the overlap has always to be defined specifically.\n\nTherefore a new approach will be added, which will be applied to partial interfaces (usually marked as `DomNoInterfaceObject` by AngleSharp): We will use static classes with extension methods for such interfaces. These classes can then be defined in external dependencies. In C# they are easily resolved via the compiler (once we have referenced the extension library and included the namespace we can use the methods). In, e.g., JavaScript (*AngleSharp.Scripting.JavaScript*) they can be resolved easily via an extension cache that is prepared with the configuration that collects all known extensions. In the long run this approach should provide a more agile basis.\n\nNow its easy to guess what is about to come: AngleSharp.Core will be split into two libraries. This is a good compromise between the previously suggested three libraries and the current state of a single library. In the end we will obtain AngleSharp.Core and AngleSharp.Css. The query selector capability will still be part of AngleSharp.Core, however, everything else related to the CSSOM will be outsourced.\n\nWith the upcoming changes in mind the changelog of v0.9.7 is not really sensational:\n\n- Fixed some bugs (#343, #325, #341, #347, #355, #358)\n- Improved cookie handling (#280, #274, #365)\n- Added a document factory (#331)\n- `EventNames`, `AttributeNames` and others are available (#330)\n- Allow setting the active document (#281)\n- Improved Xamarin.iOS build (#85)\n- Changed service API slightly (#157)\n- Enhanced CoreCLR support (#270, #362)\n\nEspecially, #157 took a longer time. Here many evaluations have been done and the previously mentioned planned changes took shape. In the future the goal is to open AngleSharp even more and allow hooking into the whole pipeline. Making something like CSS work without being integrated into the core will certainly help to find the right level of customization.\n\nThanks to every contributor for the input and hard work - much appreciated as always!"},"/blog/2016-09-04-anglesharp-v098-released":{"title":"AngleSharp v0.9.8 released","date":"2016-09-04 17:40:00","categories":"news release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2016-09-04-anglesharp-v098-released","keywords":["title","date","categories","news","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl","section"],"link":"/blog/2016-09-04-anglesharp-v098-released","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp v0.9.8 released\"\ndate: 2016-09-04 17:40:00\ncategories: news release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nYesterday version 0.9.8 of AngleSharp has been released. There are not too many API changes - and barely any breaking change so one should not notice anything.\n\nThe biggest changes are the improvements in the parser, the cookie handling, and the requester. Here we managed to come up with a lot of useful improvements. Furthermore, more of the API has been opened. With the v0.9.9 release we will introduce some breaking changes that are necessary to prepare the v1.0 release. It is yet unclear if there won't be a v0.9.10. Right now, the tendency is to come up with the first stable and production ready release soon.\n\nIn order to be production ready AngleSharp will separate most of the CSS into its own project (with its own versioning and maintenance). This will help in many aspects. However, we fully understand that AngleSharp is that much loved due to the fact of being *complete*, i.e., integrated so tightly with CSS. Therefore, a tiny CSS core will remain, which is responsible for parsing and building the CSSOM. Furthermore, the selectors will also remain in the core. This should be the best of both worlds.\n\nSo what will transition to its own CSS base? Essentially, all properties. Additionally, some special rules and declarations. Finally, new stuff such as the rendering device or anything related to specific properties or media queries will be placed there. Overall this should simplify the structure in AngleSharp and reduce its size.\n\nThe CSS parser will remain in AngleSharp. However, it will only return a generalized version of the CSSOM, which should be much easier to handle. Also in most cases it should be the best one to work against.\n\nThe exact changes are all listed for v0.9.8 in the changelog.\n"},"/blog/2016-10-03-anglesharp-scripting-v050-released":{"title":"AngleSharp Scripting v0.5.0 released","date":"2016-10-03 11:45:00","categories":"news release scripting","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2016-10-03-anglesharp-scripting-v050-released","keywords":["title","anglesharp","date","categories","news","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl"],"link":"/blog/2016-10-03-anglesharp-scripting-v050-released","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp Scripting v0.5.0 released\"\ndate: 2016-10-03 11:45:00\ncategories: news release scripting\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nJust now the latest version of the JavaScript scripting extension *AngleSharp.Scripting.JavaScript* was released. In the new release with the version 0.5.0 a couple of bug fixes and improvements have been placed. All in all this is certainly leading the way forward.\n\nThe biggest internal change is the placement of the correct prototype chain. This was on the list for quite some time. The change allows calling, e.g., `Object.getPrototypeOf(document)` with the result being the `HTMLDocument.prototype` element. Also objects such as `HTMLDocument` are now available on the `window` context. These objects are the constructor functions of their matching DOM interfaces. In most cases the constructors are just dummies, which cannot be used directly.\n\nA quite nice addition is the first API extension from the library. The `ExecuteScript` extension method works on document instances. It allows direct evaluation of a JavaScript snippet. The result is a .NET object, e.g., `document.ExecuteScript(\"2+3\")` would result in a boxed `System.Double` instance (`5`). Similarly, a call to `document.ExecuteScript(\"document.querySelector('div')\")` would return the instance of the first `div` element in the document.\n\nFinally, a couple of improvements to the type casting abilities have been integrated. Functions are now also accepted in form of strings (e.g., for `setTimeout` or `setInterval`). Furthermore, for primitive types (strings, booleans, ...) the corresponding JavaScript conversion is applied. This is all done as it should be.\n\nFor the way forward we will improve the extensibility and standard complience of *AngleSharp.Scripting.JavaScript*. With the v0.6 release a set of nasty tests needs to be passed, which all work in standard browsers. The ultimate goal is to reach the v1 milestone shortly after the *AngleSharp.Core* library reaches it.\n"},"/blog/2018-01-03-anglesharp-v099-released":{"title":"AngleSharp v0.9.9.1 released","date":"2018-01-03 16:01:00","categories":"news release hotfix","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2018-01-03-anglesharp-v099-released","keywords":["title","released","date","categories","news","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl"],"link":"/blog/2018-01-03-anglesharp-v099-released","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp v0.9.9.1 released\"\ndate: 2018-01-03 16:01:00\ncategories: news release hotfix\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nFor over a year the development of AngleSharp was stale. But the project is not dead! Today I am proud to release a hotfix to the very successful version v0.9.9, called v0.9.9.1. This hotfix contains some critical fixes and improvements. It also paves the way for the v0.10 release, which is stuck with an observer problem (namely how to attach dynamic listeners to attribute changes for - from the perspective of AngleSharp.Core - unknown attributes), which is solved in this hotfix. In the long term this also enables users to disable the dynamic DOM, resulting in even better performance for static analysis when a fully dynamic DOM is not needed.\n\nOne of the things that kept AngleSharp stale was the issue of the broken tool chain. Essentially, .NET Core broke earlier behavior and as there was no global.json available the latest SDK was used by AppVeyor. Thus our build was not working (on AppVeyor, or many newer platforms that missed the required SDK) anymore. Now we specify the right SDK and also fully support Linux builds (at least WSL compatible), which had some minor issues previously.\n\nAngleSharp is already quite in use and popular these days. The NuGet statistic of downloads per day also confirms this.\n\n![AngleSharp downloads per day](../../images/nuget_stat_day.png)\n\nMore importantly, the overall number of downloads is pretty much constantly increasing. Thus we do not see any decline in AngleSharp's usage. However, increasing the number of downloads per day (i.e., stepping back in the arena with HtmlAgilityPack) will be an important goal for 2018.\n\n![AngleSharp downloads in total](../../images/nuget_stat_total.png)\n\nTo achieve this goal we will try to finally publish v0.10. If this can be done in the first half of 2018 then the way for v1 is definitely free. For v1 we will try to improve / realize\n\n- a better JS engine integration (e.g., capable of Angular, React, ...),\n- finished AngleSharp.Css (at least API-wise),\n- more complete AngleSharp.Io package (including file and directory upload options etc.),\n- a very simple renderer, and\n- a very complete NuGet package that hosts everything combined (AngleSharp.Browser).\n\nLet's see what can be done! Happy new year everyone and thanks for using AngleSharp."},"/blog/2018-03-09-taking-anglesharp-to-the-next-level":{"title":"Taking AngleSharp to the Next Level","date":"2018-03-09 10:15:00","categories":"news organization","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2018-03-09-taking-anglesharp-to-the-next-level","keywords":["title","taking","level","date","categories","news","organization","author","florian","rappl"],"link":"/blog/2018-03-09-taking-anglesharp-to-the-next-level","content":"---\ntitle: \"Taking AngleSharp to the Next Level\"\ndate: 2018-03-09 10:15:00\ncategories: news organization\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nIn the recent two months some advancements have been made to ensure that AngleSharp will see active development also in the future. Today I am happy to announce that plans for joining the .NET Foundation are becoming more solid.\n\nThe current progress of this endeavour can be trakced [in the GitHub issue of the AngleSharp.Core repository](https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp/issues/648). Many steps are missing, but its a start.\n\nAlso to give an update on our current timetable. Currently, the roadmap looks as follows:\n\n- A second hotfix for AngleSharp v0.9.9 will be released within the next (0.9.9.2); dealing with the ConditionalWeakTable (thanks for the PR!)\n- The big new v0.10 release should be out end of March / beginning of April (keep our fingers crossed) - together with it the first release of AngleSharp.Css is expected\n- AngleSharp.Scripting will see much dedication towards the middle of the year; expect to run Angular.js, React, Angular, any jQuery version etc. with it - we will try to make this all possible\n- Potentially towards EO Q3 2018 AngleSharp v0.11 will happen\n- Hopefully towards the end of the year we finally have v1\n\nJust some remarks on v0.11: Yes, the plan was to release v1 directly after v0.10, however, that plan is not realistic. First of all, there may be some issues that go beyond a hotfix (API changes). v0.10 has (unfortunately way too many) API changes, which need to be carefully assessed first. However, that alone does not justify a v0.11.\n\nThe most important aspect for v0.11 will be the drop of all legacy systems. Right now AngleSharp is delivered with multiple libraries inside the same NuGet package (one lib targetting .NET 4, another one for SL, PCL, ...). This will change - we will only target .NET Standard (either 1.0 or 1.3). The decision for the specific .NET Standard version is not yet done, but there are many arguments in favor of 1.3.\n\nUltimately, our goal is to make development of AngleSharp simpler and to provide a more robust and streamlined version to our users. Going for .NET Standard (1.3) is not only done for cosmetics, but involves an internal rewrite with `Span<T>` and `Memory<T>`. All in all we expect some serious performance improvements in parsing scenarios.\n"},"/blog/2019-01-06-anglesharp-v010-released":{"title":"AngleSharp v0.10.0 released","date":"2019-01-06 10:49:00","categories":"news release major","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2019-01-06-anglesharp-v010-released","keywords":["title","date","categories","news","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl","section"],"link":"/blog/2019-01-06-anglesharp-v010-released","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp v0.10.0 released\"\ndate: 2019-01-06 10:49:00\ncategories: news release major\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nAfter more than 2 years AngleSharp v0.10 has finally become a reality. Jointly with AngleSharp.Io and AngleSharp.Css we now enter the v0.10 release cycle - an important milestone before hitting v1. The next step is an intermediate v0.11 (potentially of all of these libraries) to include feedback, suggestions, fixes, and other features before becoming stable.\n\nAngleSharp started to be an HTML5 parser with a fully implemented (W3C compatible) DOM in mind. Now AngleSharp includes hooks to allow anything from custom requesters, cookie management, URL patterns, web extensions, styling engines, script engines, media handlers, and document handlers. If you want to handle some web resource in .NET (e.g., an HTML document, a CSS stylesheet, ...) AngleSharp should be in your mind.\n\nI want to take the chance to say **thank you** to all contributors, users, and fans of AngleSharp. It is truly an outstanding community effort and hopefully the beginning of a on ongoing effort to build the best possible eco-system for web technology analysis / usage in .NET. Thanks for all the patience and continuous feedback!\n\nWe already started with a renewed documentation currently available via [the GitHub repository](https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp/blob/master/doc/index.md). This will soon be also available on the website and distributed to all repositories.\n\nEspecially interesting is the [available migration guide](https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp/blob/master/doc/Migration.md). It is currently not 100% complete, but we try to extend it soon. While some namespaces and namings may have changed (slightly) the biggest change is the removal of the old .NET 4 and Silverlight targets. Yes, this will certainly hurt some people, but keeping these targets alive has become more and more difficult and time-consuming. It was one of the major points that prevented AngleSharp v0.10 to be released earlier. Now the time is right to fully adhere to .NET Standard.\n\nBesides some interesting features in the CSS value model we now want to focus on the all-new *AngleSharp.Js*. For this we need to jointly improve (and extend) Jint as Jint v3 seems to be not only the future of Jint, but also a great improvement (e.g., inlcuding ES6) over good old Jint v2. Any help is appreciated (either directly on AngleSharp, a related project such as AngleSharp.Js, or Jint). Let's build this together!\n"},"/blog/2019-02-12-anglesharp-v011-released":{"title":"AngleSharp v0.11.0 released","date":"2019-02-12 09:20:00","categories":"news release major","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2019-02-12-anglesharp-v011-released","keywords":["title","date","categories","news","major","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl"],"link":"/blog/2019-02-12-anglesharp-v011-released","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp v0.11.0 released\"\ndate: 2019-02-12 09:20:00\ncategories: news release major\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nYesterday the latest version of AngleSharp has been released. This version corrects some smaller issues of the big 0.10 release published a month ago. It also brings some further advancements such as improved fragment parsing, some internal bugfixes, or the removal of AngleSharp.Xml from the core library.\n\nThe removal of AngleSharp.Xml has been done for several reasons. For once, not many people have been interested in or actively using this part of AngleSharp. The other reason is that this way AngleSharp.Xml can have its own release cycle and expand beyond the constraint feature set determined by AngleSharp.Core. This logic closely follows the logic for extracting AngleSharp.Css out of the core.\n\nAs a first consequence the `AutoSelectedMarkupFormatter` has been removed from AngleSharp.Core. It now lives in AngleSharp.Xml as the `XhtmlMarkupFormatter` is also part of AngleSharp.Xml.\n\nFinally, this release also expands the documentation with more information regarding the migration steps and examples to get started.\n"},"/blog/2019-05-02-dotnet-foundation":{"title":"Joining the .NET Foundation","date":"2019-05-02 15:00:00","categories":"news organization release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2019-05-02-dotnet-foundation","keywords":["title","joining","date","categories","news","organization","author","florian","rappl","userid"],"link":"/blog/2019-05-02-dotnet-foundation","content":"---\ntitle: \"Joining the .NET Foundation\"\ndate: 2019-05-02 15:00:00\ncategories: news organization release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nToday version v0.12 of AngleSharp has been published. Like v0.11 this is only an intermediate release before becoming officially stable with v1.0. Obviously, I could now write a bit on the bugfixes, enhancements, and additions that come with this release. However, there is a bigger organizational change that has finally happend with the release of v0.12 ...\n\n... with this release AngleSharp is a member of the .NET Foundation. We will be supported with everything we need to sustain development on this project, bringing it to the next level and provide first-class web tooling to any .NET project. The vision of AngleSharp is now officially supported by the .NET Foundation!\n\n> The long term vision for AngleSharp is provide all the building blocks for downloading, inspecting, running, and rendering standard web assets within .NET applications.\n\nAngleSharp is a .NET Browser Engine Core, which represents the basis for modern web tooling available to .NET applications in form of a .NET Standard library. This implies not only development time spent on AngleSharp itself, but also on related libraries and tools, e.g., Jint or Esprima.NET.\n\nThe hope that comes with this organizational change is a bit of momentum to enhance the AngleSharp community. The vision is too large to be fulfilled by a few people - we need a strong community that desires and believes in strong web tooling for .NET.\n\nThanks to the .NET Foundation for their support. Let's follow the journey towards outstanding web tooling in .NET together!\n"},"/blog/2019-05-06-anglesharp-v012-forward":{"title":"AngleSharp v0.12 and the future","date":"2019-05-06 22:23:10","categories":"news organization release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2019-05-06-anglesharp-v012-forward","keywords":["title","future","categories","news","organization","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl"],"link":"/blog/2019-05-06-anglesharp-v012-forward","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp v0.12 and the future\"\ndate: 2019-05-06 22:23:10\ncategories: news organization release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nLast week the latest version of AngleSharp v0.12 has been released. Not only the core library was updated to v0.12, but we also started updating some related libraries. So far we released:\n\n- AngleSharp.Xml\n- AngleSharp.Io\n\nBoth contain several improvements. For instance, AngleSharp.Xml now supports fragments. This means that changing properties such as `InnerHtml` also work in an `IXmlDocument` document. Also the XML parser contains a special fragment parsing mode. Besides the fragment parsing the source reference option also was included in the XML parser.\n\nAngleSharp.Io now comes with an advanced cookie provider - written fully from scratch it should come with much less quirks than the `MemoryCookieProvider`, which used the .NET `CookieContainer`. The new provider fully adheres to the RFC 6265 specification. Without much hacks it already deals with all the cookies that required sanitizations and other tricks to be working with the standard .NET implementation. Even better - its internal representation `WebCookie` can be used also outside of AngleSharp. The new cookie provider (called `AdvancedCookieProvider`) also supports syncing to the file system. Here the de-facto standard Netscape format has been implemented.\n\nAngleSharp.Io also introduces additional helpers to simplify downloading response content (potentially even automatic, e.g., when following a link to a binary) or appending files to a form. The new `AppendFile` helper for an `IHtmlInputElement` can work with a stream or local file path.\n\nNevertheless, two important libraries are missing from the list above:\n\n- AngleSharp.Css\n- AngleSharp.Js\n\nThe CSS library will be updated to AngleSharp v0.12 first (earliest estimated release date: 15th of May). This is currently in progress and while the library itself will still not be \"finished\" yet, this version should represent a significant step forward. In this release much of the CSSOM regarding values will be opened (e.g., `ICssFunctionValue`) and equipped with helpers to traverse the CSSOM easily. The serialization (especially decomposition and recombination of shorthand / longhand declarations) will be refined and improved.\n\nAfter the CSS library v0.12 is done we will prepare a short and simple release of AngleSharp.Js v0.12. This will be a compatibility release only - no significant changes or improvements are planned here. Our goal here is to make existing code / examples work with AngleSharp v0.12 as in pre v0.10 versions.\n\nThe reason for such a \"simple\" compatibility release is stated in [#51](https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Js/issues/51):\n\n> Biggest road blocker for AngleSharp.Js at the moment is Jint. While awesome at its core it also brought / brings some limitations. That Jint v3 takes so long (if it ever finishes) is also not good. We need / want ES6 support and a leaner model that also has the potential to perform better. We think that this is a general .NET problem (lacking a great JS engine - something which should have been IronJS, but wasn't), which is why there is homework to be picked up before solving AngleSharp.Js.\n\nWe want to see how to proceed. Right now we have the following options:\n\n- Look for an alternative to Jint (Jint still seems like the best option)\n- Collaborate on improving Jint (could be problematic due to Jint's design)\n- Collaborate on Esprima.NET and create a new project for a JS.NET engine\n- Fully invest in a new JS.NET engine from scratch\n\nPotentially, as beforehand, the last option may be way too large. Currently, it seems that option 2 or 3 are most viable. A detailed analysis of Jint's design and codebase may give us more indication. Even more important is the repository activity there. So far it seems to be in a stale state as AngleSharp was months ago, which would not be the ideal basis to utilize for this option.\n\nAs for AngleSharp itself - we still want to hit v1 this year (finally!). More full releases (e.g., v0.13) are therefore expected.\n"},"/blog/2019-05-15-anglesharp-v0121":{"title":"AngleSharp v0.12.1","date":"2019-05-15 12:52:00","categories":"news release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2019-05-15-anglesharp-v0121","keywords":["title","date","categories","news","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl","section"],"link":"/blog/2019-05-15-anglesharp-v0121","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp v0.12.1\"\ndate: 2019-05-15 12:52:00\ncategories: news release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nYesterday a small hotfix release for AngleSharp v0.12 was released. With AngleSharp v0.12.1 also all the major libraries have been updated to v0.12.1 as well:\n\n- AngleSharp.Xml\n- AngleSharp.Io\n- AngleSharp.Css\n- AngleSharp.Js\n\nThe hotfix corrected the assembly version of AngleSharp, which was still delivered at 0.11. An update to the build script should prevent such issues in the future.\n\nWe used the hotfix to also deliver some smaller enhancements, such as additions to the `Accessors` flags or an improvement to the `TaskEventLoop`.\n\nIn AngleSharp.Css we could add some missing attributes. In AngleSharp.Xml we fixed the namespace for the `WithXml` from `AngleSharp.Xml` to `AngleSharp`.\n\nWe also updated most of the sample repositories. In the next release we will revamp the documentation.\n"},"/blog/2020-03-13-anglesharp-v0140":{"title":"AngleSharp v0.14.0","date":"2020-03-13 07:13:00","categories":"news release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2020-03-13-anglesharp-v0140","keywords":["title","date","categories","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl","section","posts"],"link":"/blog/2020-03-13-anglesharp-v0140","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp v0.14.0\"\ndate: 2020-03-13 07:13:00\ncategories: news release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nIn the past few months it was a bit more quiet in the whole AngleSharp ecosystem. There have been a couple of reasons for this, but most importantly: **AngleSharp has reached maturity and is ready for going into 1.0**.\n\nThis is a big news. But why are we then reporting a release of 0.14?\n\nFor any software to be really mature the documentation plays a crucial role. We are aware that AngleSharp's documentation is *unfortunately* not *that* great. We'll try to improve this before going into 1.0.\n\nOne of the challenges with a proper documentation for AngleSharp is that we use no mono repo for AngleSharp. This challenge bites us in several ways. We could, actually change this, but for now we'll keep the different repositories as is. Instead, we'll solve the documentation part via microfrontends.\n\nIn the next two weeks our homepage will be changed to use [Piral](https://piral.io). All different modules will be loaded via pilets.\n\n- One module for each library (containing documentation and more infos)\n- One module for the **blog posts**\n- One module for the **playground**\n\nYou read right - we will bring in a playground. This playground will be based on **Blazor**.\n\nThe advantage is that each repository can independently trigger a documentation update. We'll use a GitHub action for this.\n\nMore on this will follow!\n\nPS: Yes, 0.14 is still a thing, but it mostly contains awesome bug fixes and improvements. Nothing really special. Why is it breaking? Because it forces `IBrowsingContext` to be `IDisposable` for cleanup purposes. Thus if you have an implementation of the `IBrowsingContext` interface somewhere that does not implement `IDisposable` you'll need to change your code there.\n"},"/blog/2026-07-25-anglesharp-css-v1":{"title":"AngleSharp.Css 1.0.0","date":"2026-07-25 17:10:00","categories":"news release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2026-07-25-anglesharp-css-v1","keywords":["title","date","categories","news","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl","section"],"link":"/blog/2026-07-25-anglesharp-css-v1","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp.Css 1.0.0\"\ndate: 2026-07-25 17:10:00\ncategories: news release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nSome milestones arrive quickly. Others... take a little longer...\n\n## 🎉 AngleSharp.Css 1.0.0 Is Finally Here!\n\nAfter **almost three years** since the last release of **AngleSharp.Css**, I'm incredibly happy to finally announce the release of **AngleSharp.Css 1.0.0**! 🚀\n\nThis release has been years in the making. In fact, it comes **more than 3.5 years after AngleSharp itself reached version 1.0.0**. During that time, many users who needed complete CSS support had to rely on preview packages while the library steadily evolved behind the scenes. It's been a long wait—but hopefully one that was worth it.\n\nTo everyone who has been using the previews, reporting bugs, suggesting improvements, or simply being patient: **thank you**. This release wouldn't have happened without your feedback and encouragement.\n\n## A Major Milestone for the CSS Engine\n\nVersion 1.0.0 isn't just a symbolic version bump—it represents a mature and stable implementation of CSS for the AngleSharp ecosystem.\n\nThe library now offers comprehensive CSS parsing capabilities together with a complete CSS Object Model (CSSOM) and support for constructing render trees. Whether you're analyzing stylesheets, building tooling, scraping websites with accurate style information, or experimenting with rendering, AngleSharp.Css now provides a solid and stable foundation.\n\nThis release also includes plenty of work that isn't immediately visible but makes a real difference:\n\n* 📚 Significantly improved documentation to help both new and experienced users.\n* ⚡ Numerous performance improvements throughout the parsing pipeline and internal data structures.\n* 🛠️ Many bug fixes and standards compliance improvements accumulated over the preview releases.\n\nAs always, there are still ideas for future enhancements, but reaching 1.0 means the API is now considered stable and ready for production use.\n\n## The AngleSharp Ecosystem Has Matured Too\n\nWhile AngleSharp.Css has been working its way toward 1.0, the rest of the ecosystem hasn't been standing still.\n\nAngleSharp itself has grown into a mature project that is used in many production applications around the world. Along the way, the project has adopted a dedicated security reporting process, helping ensure that security issues can be handled responsibly and efficiently.\n\nAnother exciting milestone was receiving support from **HeroDevs' Open Source Sustainability Fund**. ❤️\n\nOpen source projects thrive because of their communities, and financial support like this makes it much easier to dedicate time to maintenance, improvements, documentation, and long-term stability. I'm extremely grateful for that support and for everyone who continues to believe in the project.\n\n## What's Next?\n\nOf course, reaching 1.0.0 doesn't mean development stops—it actually makes it easier to move even faster.\n\nThere are already several exciting milestones on the roadmap:\n\n* 🌐 **AngleSharp.Js** reaching its own 1.0 release.\n* 🕸️ **AngleSharp.Wasm** following with a stable 1.0 version.\n* 🎨 A renderer built on top of AngleSharp.Css, bringing the ecosystem one step closer to a fully managed rendering pipeline.\n\nThere will also be more regular releases going forward. Now that the foundation is in place, future updates can focus on incremental improvements, new standards support, performance optimizations, and ecosystem enhancements.\n\n## Supporting AngleSharp\n\nAngleSharp has always been an open source project driven by the community. Every issue report, pull request, discussion, and recommendation has helped make the project what it is today.\n\nIf your company or your own projects benefit from AngleSharp, please consider sponsoring its continued development. Even small recurring contributions help justify spending time on maintenance, bug fixes, documentation, and new features.\n\nYou can support the project here:\n\n* GitHub Sponsors: [github.com/sponsors/FlorianRappl](https://github.com/sponsors/FlorianRappl)\n* Buy Me a Coffee: [buymeacoffee.com/florianrappl](https://buymeacoffee.com/florianrappl)\n\nEvery contribution is genuinely appreciated. 🙏\n\n## Thank You\n\nLooking back, it's amazing to see how far the AngleSharp ecosystem has come.\n\nWhat started as a standards-compliant HTML parser has steadily grown into a comprehensive collection of libraries for working with modern web technologies in .NET. Reaching **AngleSharp.Css 1.0.0** is another major step along that journey, and it's one I've been looking forward to sharing for quite some time.\n\nThank you to everyone who has contributed code, opened issues, tested preview releases, sponsored the project, or simply built something awesome with AngleSharp.\n\nHere's to the next chapter of the ecosystem—and happy parsing! 🚀\n"},"/blog/2026-07-27-anglesharp-v160":{"title":"AngleSharp 1.6.0","date":"2026-07-27 00:15:00","categories":"news release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2026-07-27-anglesharp-v160","keywords":["title","date","categories","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl","section","posts"],"link":"/blog/2026-07-27-anglesharp-v160","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp 1.6.0\"\ndate: 2026-07-27 00:15:00\ncategories: news release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nIt's time for another release! I'm happy to announce that **AngleSharp 1.6.0** is now available.\n\nWhile this release contains plenty of improvements to the core library itself, it's also a good opportunity to look at the bigger picture. There's been a lot of movement across the entire AngleSharp ecosystem recently, and it feels like everything is coming together nicely.\n\n## 🚀 AngleSharp 1.6.0: A Stronger Foundation for the Ecosystem\n\nThe biggest news is undoubtedly the release of **AngleSharp.Css 1.0.0**. After years of preview releases and continuous improvements, the CSS library has finally reached its first stable milestone.\n\nThat also means the ecosystem now offers a production-ready HTML parser together with a fully mature CSS implementation—a combination many users have been waiting for.\n\nAnd that's not where the story ends...\n\nRecently, **AngleSharp.Js** has started receiving renewed attention as well. The goal is clear: bring it to its own **1.0.0** milestone and complete another important piece of the ecosystem. There's still work to do, but it's exciting to see momentum building again.\n\nIt's fair to say that the AngleSharp ecosystem is more active than it has been in quite some time. 🎉\n\n## AngleSharp 1.6.0\n\nOf course, this release isn't just about the surrounding projects.\n\nAngleSharp itself continues to evolve with a focus on standards compliance, correctness, performance, and providing a pleasant API for developers building everything from web scrapers to static site generators, testing frameworks, analyzers, and rendering solutions.\n\nVersion **1.6.0** brings several meaningful improvements.\n\n### Faster Under the Hood ⚡\n\nPerformance has always been an important goal for AngleSharp, and this release continues that trend.\n\nA number of internal optimizations reduce overhead and improve throughput across common parsing scenarios. While many of these improvements aren't immediately visible from the outside, they contribute to making AngleSharp an even better choice for high-performance workloads.\n\n## Smarter URL Handling\n\nOne of the more interesting additions in this release is an expansion of the `Url` class.\n\nAngleSharp now performs much more comprehensive parsing and normalization of **IPv4** and **IPv6** addresses, following the URL specification more closely.\n\nThat includes support for various numeric representations of IPv4 addresses—including decimal, hexadecimal, octal, and mixed-digit formats—which are all normalized to their canonical representation. While these formats aren't something most developers encounter every day, supporting them correctly is an important part of standards compliance and compatibility with browser behavior.\n\nIt's one of those features that most people will never notice—but when you need it, you'll be glad it's there.\n\n## Better CSS Selector Support\n\nThe CSS selector engine has also received additional improvements.\n\nSelectors are at the heart of many AngleSharp use cases, and every enhancement here benefits developers working with HTML analysis, scraping, testing, document transformations, and much more.\n\nAs browser standards continue to evolve, keeping selector support robust and performant remains a priority.\n\n## Looking Beyond AngleSharp\n\nOne particularly exciting development is a project that has started to grow just outside the AngleSharp ecosystem while relying heavily on it under the hood: [**netpack**](https://github.com/FlorianRappl/netpack).\n\nIf you haven't heard of it yet, netpack aims to bring a modern, high-performance asset bundler to the .NET world.\n\nUnlike traditional JavaScript tooling—which typically revolves around npm, Node.js, and a collection of external tools—netpack is built entirely in **.NET**. It can certainly fit into modern web development workflows, but its real strength is that it's also available as a fully managed library.\n\nImagine being able to:\n\n* understand HTML, CSS, and JavaScript assets,\n* optimize and transform them,\n* bundle everything together,\n* and serve the results,\n\nall without ever leaving the .NET ecosystem.\n\nThat's the vision behind netpack, and AngleSharp provides many of the standards-compliant parsing capabilities that make it possible.\n\nThe project is still taking shape, but it's already exciting to see where it's heading. If you're curious, it's definitely worth keeping an eye on.\n\n👉 [netpack.anglevisions.com](https://netpack.anglevisions.com/)\n\n## Supporting AngleSharp\n\nAngleSharp has always been an open source project driven by the community. Every issue report, pull request, discussion, and recommendation has helped make the project what it is today.\n\nIf your company or your own projects benefit from AngleSharp, please consider sponsoring its continued development. Even small recurring contributions help justify spending time on maintenance, bug fixes, documentation, and new features.\n\nYou can support the project here:\n\n* GitHub Sponsors: [github.com/sponsors/FlorianRappl](https://github.com/sponsors/FlorianRappl)\n* Buy Me a Coffee: [buymeacoffee.com/florianrappl](https://buymeacoffee.com/florianrappl)\n\nEvery contribution is genuinely appreciated. 🙏\n\n## More to Come\n\nBetween **AngleSharp 1.6.0**, **AngleSharp.Css 1.0.0**, renewed progress on **AngleSharp.Js**, and emerging projects like **netpack**, there's a lot happening right now.\n\nThe long-term vision remains unchanged: provide a complete, standards-compliant toolkit for understanding and working with modern web technologies entirely within .NET.\n\nEvery release moves that vision a little further forward.\n\nAs always, thank you to everyone who has contributed issues, pull requests, ideas, sponsorship, and feedback. Open source only moves forward because of its community, and it's incredibly rewarding to see AngleSharp continue to grow after all these years.\n\nHere's to the next release—and there's plenty more on the roadmap. 🚀\n"},"/blog/2026-08-01-anglesharp-js-v100-release":{"title":"AngleSharp.Js 1.0.0","date":"2026-08-01 13:15:00","categories":"news release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2026-08-01-anglesharp-js-v100-release","keywords":["title","date","categories","news","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl","section"],"link":"/blog/2026-08-01-anglesharp-js-v100-release","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp.Js 1.0.0\"\ndate: 2026-08-01 13:15:00\ncategories: news release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nAnother long-awaited milestone has finally arrived:\n\n**AngleSharp.Js 1.0.0 is here!** 🚀\n\nWith this release, the final major building block of the AngleSharp ecosystem reaches its first stable version. Together with **AngleSharp 1.6.0** and **AngleSharp.Css 1.0.0**, it marks an important moment for the project: AngleSharp has evolved from \"just\" an HTML parser into a comprehensive platform for understanding, executing, and interacting with modern web applications—all from .NET.\n\n## JavaScript, the Way the Web Uses It\n\nThe modern web doesn't stop at HTML.\n\nWhether you're dealing with React, Angular, Vue, jQuery, or plain JavaScript, websites increasingly depend on client-side execution before they reveal their true content. Static parsing alone often isn't enough anymore.\n\nThat's exactly where AngleSharp.Js comes in.\n\nBy integrating a high-performance JavaScript engine directly into the AngleSharp ecosystem, AngleSharp.Js enables real-world websites to be evaluated inside .NET while seamlessly interacting with the DOM.\n\nThe result is a browsing experience that feels remarkably close to what happens inside a real browser—but entirely managed, extensible, and embeddable within your own application.\n\n## Powered by Jint ❤️\n\nNone of this would be possible without the incredible work done by the team behind **Jint**.\n\nOver the years, they have built what is arguably one of the best JavaScript interpreters available for .NET, with excellent ECMAScript support—including full ES6 compatibility—and an impressive focus on correctness and performance.\n\nJust as importantly, they've been fantastic collaborators.\n\nA huge amount of work has gone into making the integration between Jint and AngleSharp as efficient as possible. Both projects have evolved together, continuously refining the bridge between the JavaScript runtime and the DOM while squeezing as much performance as possible out of the architecture.\n\nThe result is an integration that feels natural instead of bolted on.\n\nA big thank you goes to everyone involved in making Jint and AngleSharp.Js what they are today.\n\n## The Complete AngleSharp Ecosystem\n\nOne of the strengths of AngleSharp has always been its modular architecture.\n\nInstead of forcing every application to include every web technology, you can assemble exactly the capabilities you need.\n\nWant to execute JavaScript?\n\n➡️ Install **AngleSharp.Js**.\n\nNeed CSS parsing, selector matching, computed styles, or render-tree support?\n\n➡️ Add **AngleSharp.Css**.\n\nNeed better networking, support for `fetch`, `XMLHttpRequest`, custom requesters, or advanced I/O integration?\n\n➡️ **AngleSharp.Io** has you covered.\n\nWorking with XML documents or validating against DTDs?\n\n➡️ That's what **AngleSharp.Xml** is for.\n\nNeed WebAssembly support?\n\n➡️ **AngleSharp.Wasm** is there for exactly that.\n\nPiece by piece, the ecosystem has grown into a toolbox that covers almost every aspect of working with web technologies in managed .NET.\n\n## What Does 1.0 Mean?\n\nVersion 1.0 is much more than changing the first digit.\n\nIt represents years of bug fixes, architectural improvements, compatibility work, and API stabilization.\n\nThis release includes a wide range of improvements across the project.\n\n### Better Performance ⚡\n\nSeveral architectural improvements make JavaScript execution faster and more scalable.\n\nOne notable optimization is the redesign of DOM prototype creation. Instead of rebuilding type information for every document, prototype layouts are now shared across the process, allowing type resolution to happen once while giving the JavaScript engine much warmer caches for member access.\n\nInternal caches have also become thread-safe, reducing overhead in concurrent scenarios.\n\n### Better Standards Compliance\n\nA lot of work has gone into making browser behavior more accurate.\n\nAmong many fixes, this release improves:\n\n* document ready-state handling\n* `location` assignment and navigation behavior\n* DOM object interoperability between C# and JavaScript\n* `NodeFilter`\n* `Window.parent`\n* `hasChildNodes()`\n* `XMLHttpRequest` handling\n* dataset synchronization between JavaScript and the underlying DOM\n\nThere are also fixes for deeply recursive scripts that previously could result in `StackOverflowException`s, making the runtime significantly more robust.\n\n### New Features\n\nVersion 1.0 also introduces several new capabilities, including:\n\n* 🌐 **Web Worker** support\n* ⚙️ configurable JavaScript engines through `JsScriptingOptions`\n* 📢 reporting of uncaught event-loop exceptions through the browsing context\n\nMeanwhile, networking-related functionality such as `Navigator` and `XmlHttpRequester` has been moved into **AngleSharp.Io**, where it naturally belongs and can evolve independently.\n\nFinally, AngleSharp.Js has been updated to build on **AngleSharp 1.x** and the latest **Jint v4**, bringing the latest improvements from both projects into one release.\n\n## What's Next? Rendering. 🖥️\n\nNow that HTML, CSS, JavaScript, networking, XML, and even WebAssembly all have dedicated libraries, the next big milestone is becoming increasingly tangible:\n\n**AngleSharp.Renderer.**\n\nThe goal is ambitious but exciting.\n\nInstead of stopping at DOM manipulation and style computation, AngleSharp.Renderer aims to provide actual rendering capabilities for the ecosystem.\n\nThat means supporting layout information such as `offsetLeft`, `offsetTop`, element dimensions, and other APIs that traditionally require a browser engine. It also opens the door to fully headless browser scenarios, screenshot generation, visual testing, and automated interaction with rendered pages.\n\nOne of the next major goals is to build an example browser showcasing these capabilities and demonstrating just how far a fully managed .NET browser stack can go.\n\nIt's an exciting direction—and one that has been years in the making.\n\n## Supporting the Project\n\nOpen source projects don't evolve like this by accident.\n\nAngleSharp, AngleSharp.Js, and the surrounding ecosystem represent thousands of hours spent implementing standards, fixing edge cases, optimizing performance, reviewing issues, and helping users.\n\nIf these libraries save you time, power your products, or simply make your life a little easier, please consider supporting their continued development.\n\nYou can sponsor the project through GitHub Sponsors or Buy Me a Coffee:\n\n* GitHub Sponsors: [github.com/sponsors/FlorianRappl](https://github.com/sponsors/FlorianRappl)\n* Buy Me a Coffee: [buymeacoffee.com/florianrappl](https://buymeacoffee.com/florianrappl)\n\nEvery contribution helps make future releases—and ambitious projects like AngleSharp.Renderer—possible. ❤️\n\n## Thank You\n\nReaching **AngleSharp.Js 1.0.0** feels like the completion of a long chapter.\n\nWhat began years ago as an HTML parser has steadily grown into a modular platform capable of parsing documents, executing JavaScript, evaluating CSS, communicating over the network, processing XML, and soon—even rendering complete web pages.\n\nThere is still plenty to build, but today is a moment worth celebrating.\n\nThank you to everyone who contributed code, filed issues, reviewed pull requests, tested previews, sponsored development, or simply trusted AngleSharp in your own projects.\n\nHere's to the next chapter of the ecosystem. 🚀\n"},"/blog/2026-11-06-anglesharp-v110-release":{"title":"AngleSharp 1.10.0","date":"2026-11-06 13:15:00","categories":"news release","author":"Florian Rappl","userId":"FlorianRappl","section":"Posts","id":"/blog/2026-11-06-anglesharp-v110-release","keywords":["title","date","categories","news","author","florian","rappl","userid","florianrappl","section"],"link":"/blog/2026-11-06-anglesharp-v110-release","content":"---\ntitle: \"AngleSharp 1.10.0\"\ndate: 2026-11-06 13:15:00\ncategories: news release\nauthor: \"Florian Rappl\"\nuserId: FlorianRappl\nsection: Posts\n---\nHappy to share that we have some big release party today:\n\n**AngleSharp 1.10.0 is here!** 🚀\n\nAngleSharp is a web utility library for .NET written exclusivly in C#. It is the de-facto standard for parsing HTML5 on the .NET platform. Through its architecture it is actually more than just an HTML parser - it is the core of a headless browser. With associated libraries such as AngleSharp.Css or AngleSharp.Js more web capabilities such as style computation or script evaluation are possible.\n\nThe v1.10 release marks an important milestone in the AngleSharp project. Many DOM APIs have been implemented that previously did not appear at all. While these APIs did not make much of a difference for standard HTML parsing they are crucial in the JavaScript evaluation of many websites.\n\n## A New Milestone for the Entire Ecosystem\n\nToday marks an important milestone for AngleSharp! We are happy to announce the release of **AngleSharp 1.10.0**, accompanied by new releases across the entire ecosystem: AngleSharp.Css 1.4.0, AngleSharp.Js 1.2.0, AngleSharp.Io 1.3.0, and AngleSharp.Renderer 0.11.0.\n\nThis is more than just another round of package updates. AngleSharp 1.10.0 represents a significant step forward for the core library and establishes a new common foundation for the ecosystem. All the latest releases of the companion libraries are built on top of AngleSharp 1.10.0 and require it as their underlying dependency.\n\nLet's take a closer look at what's new and why this release is particularly exciting.\n\n## AngleSharp 1.10.0 – The Foundation\n\n[AngleSharp 1.10.0](https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp/releases/tag/v1.10.0) is the centerpiece of this release cycle.\n\nAs the foundation on which the other libraries build, improvements to AngleSharp have an impact that reaches far beyond the core package itself. Better DOM behavior, more complete browser API implementations, improved security, and additional standards support benefit the entire ecosystem.\n\nThere are several noteworthy additions and improvements.\n\n### Better DOM and browser API compatibility\n\nThis release brings a considerable number of improvements to DOM behavior and browser API compatibility.\n\nAmong the highlights are:\n\n- **Custom Elements:** Support for the `customElements` registry, including `define`, `get`, `getName`, `whenDefined`, and `upgrade`, as well as the `:defined` pseudo-class.\n- **Event handling:** Support for the `capture`, `once`, `passive`, and `signal` options in `addEventListener` and `removeEventListener`.\n- **DOM improvements:** Added `isConnected`, `toggleAttribute`, `replaceChildren`, `getRootNode`, and several other useful DOM APIs.\n- **Form improvements:** Added `requestSubmit`, `reportValidity`, and `SubmitEvent`, including form validation, cancellation, and submitter reporting.\n- **Popover API:** Added support for the `popover` attribute.\n- **Pointer and keyboard events:** Expanded event support with `PointerEvent`, additional keyboard properties, and improved mouse event information.\n\nThere are also numerous correctness fixes, including improvements to live DOM collections, `TreeWalker`, frame initialization, event propagation, and document writing.\n\nThese changes make AngleSharp behave more consistently with the browser platform and provide a stronger foundation for applications that depend on accurate DOM semantics.\n\n### Security improvements\n\nSecurity remains an important consideration when implementing browser behavior outside an actual browser.\n\nAngleSharp 1.10.0 adds support for the `sandbox` attribute on iframes, applying the corresponding security restrictions to their browsing contexts.\n\nIt also introduces support for `X-Frame-Options` and the `frame-ancestors` directive of Content Security Policy. These mechanisms help prevent documents from being displayed in frames when their embedding policies prohibit it.\n\nThese additions strengthen the security model around embedded documents and bring AngleSharp closer to the behavior expected from modern browsers.\n\n### More control over document creation\n\nTwo new document creation options deserve special attention:\n\n- `CreateDocumentOptions.IsScriptingDisabled`\n- `CreateDocumentOptions.IsNavigationDisabled`\n\nThey provide more precise control over document creation, including scenarios such as DOM parsing where scripting and navigation should not be enabled.\n\nTogether with the other improvements, these additions make AngleSharp more flexible for different embedding and processing scenarios.\n\n**In short:** AngleSharp 1.10.0 is a substantial improvement to the core platform, combining standards compatibility, better DOM behavior, security enhancements, and additional browser APIs.\n\n## AngleSharp.Css 1.4.0 – Better Styling and Rendering\n\n[AngleSharp.Css 1.4.0](https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Css/releases/tag/v1.4.0) brings improvements to CSS processing and its integration with the DOM.\n\nImportantly, this release now requires at least AngleSharp 1.10.0.\n\nSome of the highlights include:\n\n- Improved rendering by creating pseudo-elements and specified styles only when needed.\n- Reduced memory consumption of render trees.\n- Improved `GetInnerText` by using the render tree, correctly skipping hidden content and accessing shadow hosts through their slots.\n- Improved presentational hints without relying on reflection.\n- Added support for `CSSStyleSheet` and `adoptedStyleSheets`.\n- Added support for `::selection` pseudo-elements.\n- Improved `getComputedStyle` and its handling of initial values.\n- Added support for the popover API through `:popover-open` and `::backdrop`.\n- Expanded CSS style property access from JavaScript, including properties such as `gap`, `aspectRatio`, `gridTemplateColumns`, and `inset`.\n\nThere are also improvements to `CSS.supports`, media query handling, and CSS supports conditions.\n\nOf particular interest are the rendering and memory improvements. Avoiding unnecessary style and pseudo-element creation, together with more efficient render trees, helps make CSS processing more economical.\n\n## AngleSharp.Js 1.2.0 – More Reliable JavaScript Integration\n\n[AngleSharp.Js 1.2.0](https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Js/releases/tag/v1.2.0) focuses on improving the integration between JavaScript and the DOM.\n\nThis release addresses several subtle but important compatibility issues.\n\nHighlights include:\n\n- Fixed the origin of frames without their own address so that they correctly inherit the origin of their creator.\n- Fixed `append`, `prepend`, and related methods to correctly turn strings into text nodes.\n- Improved prototype-chain behavior for classes derived from DOM types.\n- Improved support for event listeners and callbacks, including objects implementing `handleEvent`.\n- Fixed promise jobs not being executed after host callbacks, which could otherwise cause `await` operations to hang.\n- Improved callback handling for APIs such as `navigator.locks.request`.\n- Fixed event listener removal and error propagation.\n- Improved the handling of DOM exceptions, dates, structured cloning, and iterable DOM collections.\n- Fixed bound functions and callable proxies not being accepted as callbacks.\n\nOne particularly important improvement concerns module loading: JavaScript modules are now loaded through the configured requesters rather than directly from the local filesystem.\n\nThis is an important step towards consistent and controlled resource loading, especially when applications provide their own resource access policies.\n\nOverall, AngleSharp.Js 1.2.0 makes JavaScript execution more predictable and improves compatibility with the browser APIs that applications expect.\n\n## AngleSharp.Io 1.3.0 – Expanded Web Platform APIs\n\n[AngleSharp.Io 1.3.0](https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Io/releases/tag/v1.3.0) brings a substantial expansion of the available web platform APIs.\n\nThe release includes several improvements to existing networking functionality, as well as a number of new APIs.\n\n### Improved networking\n\n`XMLHttpRequest` now reads response bodies in chunks. Progress events are fired approximately every 50 milliseconds, providing updated `loaded` and `total` values when the content length is available. The `responseText` also grows while the request is in the `LOADING` state.\n\nThe final event sequence of `XMLHttpRequest` has also been corrected to follow the expected order.\n\nOther improvements include better WebSocket error reporting and a nesting limit for JSON parsing to handle deeply nested documents more safely.\n\n### More browser APIs\n\nSome particularly interesting additions include:\n\n- `Request`, `Headers`, and `Response` constructors and additional associated functionality.\n- IndexedDB support for JavaScript, including transactions, indexes, cursors, keys, and structured values.\n- `Blob`, `File`, and `FileReader`.\n- Readable, writable, and transform streams.\n- `TextEncoderStream` and `TextDecoderStream`.\n- `CompressionStream` and `DecompressionStream`.\n- Streaming response bodies.\n- `AbortController` and `AbortSignal`, including cancellation support for `fetch`.\n- `ProgressEvent` and upload progress events.\n- File System Access API support through `showOpenFilePicker` and `showSaveFilePicker`.\n- Additional service worker capabilities, including background sync and push management.\n- Notification support with an injectable delivery platform.\n\nThere are also improvements to RTC ICE server URL handling and cookie validation.\n\nOne important security fix concerns cookie paths containing control characters. Such paths are now rejected, preventing unwanted line injection into cookie files.\n\nThis release makes the I/O package considerably more capable and brings its API surface closer to what modern web applications expect.\n\n## AngleSharp.Renderer 0.11.0 – Taking Rendering Further\n\n[AngleSharp.Renderer 0.11.0](https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Renderer/releases/tag/v0.11.0) continues to improve the rendering capabilities built on top of AngleSharp.\n\nThe release brings several important layout, geometry, and interaction improvements.\n\nHighlights include:\n\n- Improved resolution of used pixel values for margins and positioned insets in `getComputedStyle`.\n- Fixed handling of mixed percentage and absolute lengths, including expressions such as `calc(50% + 10px)` and `min(240px, 55%)`.\n- Improved grid track calculations involving expressions with more than two terms.\n- Fixed scroll dimension calculations involving positioned and clipped content.\n- Added `elementFromPoint` and `elementsFromPoint`.\n- Expanded mouse event geometry with `pageX`, `pageY`, `offsetX`, and `offsetY`.\n- Added more used values to `getComputedStyle`, including dimensions, padding, border widths, and inherited text properties.\n- Added text selection support, including the DOM's `getSelection()` API and the corresponding selection events.\n- Added `ResizeObserver` and `IntersectionObserver`.\n- Added `Range.getClientRects()` and `Range.getBoundingClientRect()`.\n- Added `element.animate()` and related Web Animations APIs using the renderer's virtual clock.\n- Integrated `document.fonts` and script-created `FontFace` objects into font matching and layout.\n\nThese additions are particularly interesting because they expand the interaction between the DOM, CSS, JavaScript, and actual layout.\n\nWith observers, text selection, geometry APIs, and animation support, the renderer continues to move beyond simply calculating layouts towards providing a richer browser-like environment.\n\n## A Stronger Ecosystem, Together\n\nOne of the most important aspects of this release cycle is the alignment of all the companion libraries.\n\nThe latest versions are:\n\n| Package | Version |\n|---|---:|\n| AngleSharp | **1.10.0** |\n| AngleSharp.Css | **1.4.0** |\n| AngleSharp.Js | **1.2.0** |\n| AngleSharp.Io | **1.3.0** |\n| AngleSharp.Renderer | **0.11.0** |\n\nAll these libraries build on top of AngleSharp, and their latest releases target AngleSharp 1.10.0 as their common foundation.\n\nThis is an important point for anyone maintaining an existing application: upgrading only one component may not be sufficient to benefit from the latest ecosystem improvements. We recommend updating the related packages together to ensure that they work with the same core version and take advantage of the improvements introduced throughout the stack.\n\nThe result is a more consistent platform, where DOM behavior, CSS processing, JavaScript execution, resource loading, and rendering can evolve together.\n\n## Thank You to the Community!\n\nNo open-source project grows in isolation, and this release cycle is a great example of that.\n\nWe would particularly like to acknowledge the contributions from **michaelthyregod**, who contributed improvements across AngleSharp.Css and AngleSharp.Io.\n\nThese contributions include performance optimizations, reduced memory usage, improvements to CSS processing, and security-related fixes.\n\nWe also appreciate everyone who reports issues, tests new releases, provides feedback, and helps improve the project.\n\nCommunity contributions are especially valuable in a project like AngleSharp, where correctness and compatibility depend on getting many small details right. A seemingly minor fix in event handling, DOM behavior, resource loading, or CSS processing can have a meaningful impact on applications built on top of the library.\n\nThe improvements in these releases reflect exactly that: a continuous effort to make the implementation more correct, more secure, and more efficient.\n\nThank you to everyone involved!\n\n## Getting Started\n\nAll five releases are available now.\n\nYou can find the packages and their detailed release notes here:\n\n- [AngleSharp 1.10.0](https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp/releases/tag/v1.10.0)\n- [AngleSharp.Css 1.4.0](https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Css/releases/tag/v1.4.0)\n- [AngleSharp.Js 1.2.0](https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Js/releases/tag/v1.2.0)\n- [AngleSharp.Io 1.3.0](https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Io/releases/tag/v1.3.0)\n- [AngleSharp.Renderer 0.11.0](https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Renderer/releases/tag/v0.11.0)\n\nIf you are using AngleSharp in an existing project, now is a great time to update.\n\n**Thank you for being part of the AngleSharp community. Here's to another milestone and many more improvements ahead!**\n\n## Supporting the Project\n\nOpen source projects don't evolve like this by accident.\n\nAngleSharp, AngleSharp.Css, AngleSharp.Io, AngleSharp.Js, AngleSharp.Renderer and the surrounding ecosystem represent thousands of hours spent implementing standards, fixing edge cases, optimizing performance, reviewing issues, and helping users.\n\nIf these libraries save you time, power your products, or simply make your life a little easier, please consider supporting their continued development.\n\nYou can sponsor the project through GitHub Sponsors or Buy Me a Coffee:\n\n* GitHub Sponsors: [github.com/sponsors/FlorianRappl](https://github.com/sponsors/FlorianRappl)\n* Buy Me a Coffee: [buymeacoffee.com/florianrappl](https://buymeacoffee.com/florianrappl)\n\nEvery contribution helps make future releases—and ambitious projects like AngleSharp.Browser—possible. ❤️\n"},"/general/introduction":{"title":"Introduction","section":"General","id":"/general/introduction","keywords":["title","section","general","ability","parse","angle","bracket","based","hyper","texts"],"link":"/general/introduction","content":"---\r\ntitle: \"Introduction\"\r\nsection: \"General\"\r\n---\r\n# Introduction\r\n\r\nAngleSharp is a .NET library that gives you the ability to parse angle bracket based hyper-texts like HTML, SVG, and MathML. XML without validation is also supported by the library. An important aspect of AngleSharp is that CSS can also be parsed. The included parser is built upon the official W3C specification. This produces a perfectly portable HTML5 DOM representation of the given source code and ensures compatibility with results in evergreen browsers. Also standard DOM features such as `querySelector` or `querySelectorAll` work for tree traversal.\r\n\r\n## Advantages\r\n\r\nThe advantage over similar libraries like *HtmlAgilityPack* is that the exposed DOM is using the official W3C specified API, i.e., that even things like `querySelectorAll` are available in AngleSharp. Also the parser uses the HTML 5.1 specification, which defines error handling and element correction. The AngleSharp library focuses on standards compliance, interactivity, and extensibility. It is therefore giving web developers working with C# all possibilities as they know from using the DOM in any modern browser.\r\n\r\nThe performance of AngleSharp is quite close to the performance of browsers. Even very large pages can be processed within milliseconds. AngleSharp tries to minimize memory allocations and reuses elements internally to avoid unnecessary object creation.\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nAngleSharp comes without any dependencies. It is created in a modular way, supporting other libraries to extend its own functionality. Examples are `AngleSharp.Css` or `AngleSharp.Io`. Some of these libraries may come with dependencies and may be constraint to certain platforms only.\r\n\r\n## Supported Platforms\r\n\r\nAngleSharp has been created as a .NET Standard 2.0 compatible library. This includes, but is not limited to:\r\n\r\n- .NET Core (2.0 and later)\r\n- .NET Framework (4.6 and later)\r\n- Xamarin.Android (7.0 and 8.0)\r\n- Xamarin.iOS (10.0 and 10.14)\r\n- Xamarin.Mac (3.0 and 3.8)\r\n- Mono (4.6 and 5.4)\r\n- UWP (10.0 and 10.0.16299)\r\n- Unity (2018.1)\r\n\r\n## .NET Foundation\r\n\r\nThis project is supported by the [.NET Foundation](https://dotnetfoundation.org).\r\n"},"/projects/Overview":{"title":"Overview","section":"General","id":"/projects/Overview","keywords":["title","overview","section","general","html","tokenizer","core","utilities","parsing","mime"],"link":"/projects/Overview","content":"---\r\ntitle: Overview\r\nsection: General\r\n---\r\n\r\n::: feature\r\n![AngleSharp.Core](../../images/anglesharp-core-logo.png) {.feature-image .img-responsive .pull-right}\r\n<h2 class=\"feature-heading\" id=\"anglesharp-core\">AngleSharp.<span class=\"text-muted\">Core</span></h2>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">HTML parser, DOM provider, CSS tokenizer, and core utilities. Helpers and parsing for mime types, URLs, source sets, and many more. Provides extension points, which are then used by the other projects.</p>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">Stable and production ready.<br><a href=\"https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp\">AngleSharp/AngleSharp</a></p>\r\n:::\r\n\r\n<hr class=\"feature-divider\" />\r\n\r\n::: feature\r\n![AngleSharp.Css](../../images/anglesharp-css-logo.png) {.feature-image .img-responsive .pull-left}\r\n<h2 class=\"feature-heading\" id=\"anglesharp-css\">AngleSharp.<span class=\"text-muted\">Css</span></h2>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">A full CSS parser and CSSOM provider to include support for stylesheets, inline styles, and style computations within AngleSharp.</p>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">Stable and production ready.<br><a href=\"https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Css\">AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Css</a></p>\r\n:::\r\n\r\n<hr class=\"feature-divider\" />\r\n\r\n::: feature\r\n![AngleSharp.Diffing](../../images/anglesharp-diffing-logo.png) {.feature-image .img-responsive .pull-right}\r\n<h2 class=\"feature-heading\" id=\"anglesharp-diffing\">AngleSharp.<span class=\"text-muted\">Diffing</span></h2>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">Makes it possible to compare two AngleSharp node lists and get a list of differences between them. Used in bUnit.</p>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">Stable and production ready.<br><a href=\"https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Diffing\">AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Diffing</a></p>\r\n:::\r\n\r\n<hr class=\"feature-divider\" />\r\n\r\n::: feature\r\n![AngleSharp.Io](../../images/anglesharp-io-logo.png) {.feature-image .img-responsive .pull-left}\r\n<h2 class=\"feature-heading\" id=\"anglesharp-io\">AngleSharp.<span class=\"text-muted\">Io</span></h2>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">IO modules and helpers, including - but not limited to - improved (and more) requesters, storage abilities, and caching mechanisms.</p>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">Stable and production ready.<br><a href=\"https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Io\">AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Io</a></p>\r\n:::\r\n\r\n<hr class=\"feature-divider\" />\r\n\r\n::: feature\r\n![AngleSharp.Js](../../images/anglesharp-js-logo.png) {.feature-image .img-responsive .pull-right}\r\n<h2 class=\"feature-heading\" id=\"anglesharp-js\">AngleSharp.<span class=\"text-muted\">Js</span></h2>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">Solution for integrating a JavaScript engine (Jint). Provides DOM bindings (and binding helpers).</p>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">Stable and production ready.<br><a href=\"https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Js\">AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Js</a></p>\r\n:::\r\n\r\n<hr class=\"feature-divider\" />\r\n\r\n::: feature\r\n![AngleSharp.XPath](../../images/anglesharp-xpath-logo.png) {.feature-image .img-responsive .pull-left}\r\n<h2 class=\"feature-heading\" id=\"anglesharp-xpath\">AngleSharp.<span class=\"text-muted\">XPath</span></h2>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">Adds the ability to select nodes via XPath queries instead of CSS selector syntax.</p>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">Considered stable.<br><a href=\"https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.XPath\">AngleSharp/AngleSharp.XPath</a></p>\r\n:::\r\n\r\n<hr class=\"feature-divider\" />\r\n\r\n::: feature\r\n![AngleSharp.Xml](../../images/anglesharp-xml-logo.png) {.feature-image .img-responsive .pull-right}\r\n<h2 class=\"feature-heading\" id=\"anglesharp-xml\">AngleSharp.<span class=\"text-muted\">Xml</span></h2>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">Support for adding DOM capabilities to XML documents. Includes DTD validation.</p>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">Stable and production ready.<br><a href=\"https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Xml\">AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Xml</a></p>\r\n:::\r\n\r\n<hr class=\"feature-divider\" />\r\n\r\n::: feature\r\n![AngleSharp.Wasm](../../images/anglesharp-wasm-logo.png) {.feature-image .img-responsive .pull-left}\r\n<h2 class=\"feature-heading\" id=\"anglesharp-wasm\">AngleSharp.<span class=\"text-muted\">Wasm</span></h2>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">Support for adding support to run WASM and interact / interpret WASM parts.</p>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">Considered stable.<br><a href=\"https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Wasm\">AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Wasm</a></p>\r\n:::\r\n\r\n<hr class=\"feature-divider\" />\r\n\r\n::: feature\r\n![AngleSharp.Renderer](../../images/anglesharp-renderer-logo.png) {.feature-image .img-responsive .pull-right}\r\n<h2 class=\"feature-heading\" id=\"anglesharp-renderer\">AngleSharp.<span class=\"text-muted\">Renderer</span></h2>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">Support for rendering the styled DOM from its render tree.</p>\r\n<p class=\"lead\">Currently upcoming. Contributions welcome.<br><a href=\"https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Renderer\">AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Renderer</a></p>\r\n:::\r\n"},"/docs/01-articles":{"title":"Introduction","section":"General","id":"/docs/01-articles","keywords":["title","introduction","section","general","project","libraries","selecting","library","part","sanitize"],"link":"/docs/01-articles","content":"---\ntitle: \"Introduction\"\nsection: \"General\"\n---\n# Articles\n\nA list of articles about the AngleSharp project and how to use AngleSharp.\n\n- [HTML Parsing Libraries - C#](https://scrapingant.com/blog/html-parsing-libraries-c-sharp)\n- [Parsing HTML: Selecting the Right Library (Part 2)](https://dzone.com/articles/parsing-html-selecting-the-right-library-part-2)\n- [Sanitize HTML snippets with AngleSharp](https://www.meziantou.net/sanitize-html-snippet-with-anglesharp.htm)\n- [Create a Simple Web Scraper in C#](https://dev.to/rachelsoderberg/create-a-simple-web-scraper-in-c-1l1m)\n- [How to parse HTML in .NET](https://scrapingant.com/blog/parse-html-dot-net)\n- [How to Parse Data with AngleSharp ](https://www.zenrows.com/blog/anglesharp)\n- [How to Use AngleSharp in C# ](https://roundproxies.com/blog/anglesharp/)\n- [C# parse HTML with AngleSharp](https://zetcode.com/csharp/anglesharp-parse-html/)\n- [AngleSharp in C#: Parsing and Scraping HTML](https://code-maze.com/csharp-parsing-html-with-anglesharp/)\n- [Video: Web Scraping in C# .NET using AngleSharp](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3vWljnYWmFQ)\n\nFeel free to submit a PR for this list if some article is missing.\n\n(Last checked and updated November 2026)\n"},"/docs/02-samples":{"title":"Samples","section":"General","id":"/docs/02-samples","keywords":["title","section","general","collection","found","repository","-","samples"],"link":"/docs/02-samples","content":"---\r\ntitle: \"Samples\"\r\nsection: \"General\"\r\n---\r\n# Samples\r\n\r\nA collection of samples can be found in our [samples repository](https://github.com/AngleSharp/AngleSharp.Demos.Samples).\r\n"}}]